PROLOGUE

"Ready or not, here I come."

She paused to listen before opening her eyes.

Silence.

The boy had found a place he was happy with, one he hadn't used recently.

She had to be sure of where he was so she could save it for last. Find the kid in thirty seconds, what the hell was she going to do for the rest of the day? 

So she'd build it up. She'd call out her every move, getting louder as she got closer and then — just before the big find, when she could already hear him breathing — she'd turn away and look somewhere else.

Under the carpet. Through the keyhole. Inside her pockets. Behind her ear. 

The boy would crack up, and she'd make a big deal about hearing a small mouse, and where could it be? When she finally got to him, she'd hug him and kiss him and he'd ask for more.

More of hiding, that is. When the boy had to seek, it didn't go well.

Last time, she'd moved the fridge just enough to squeeze behind it. He went by a couple of times but never thought to look there. That was the end of the game.

"Mommy! Mommy!"

She felt terrible. She'd wait until he came back to jump out and surprise him, but he just dropped to the carpet in the living room and bawled his eyes out. She picked him up and said it was okay, Mommy was there. She even took him to the fridge and showed him where she had been, but it took cartoons and a bottle to calm him down.

That was almost a year ago, but she still wouldn't risk it.

When she finally got to him this time, curled up inside the cupboard, he said "again, Mommy!", and she squeezed him tight in a shower of kisses and went back for the next round.

The boy watched her go.

Where, now? 

Where?

Not the kitchen again.

The living room? But Mommy's there.

Bedroom.

Under the bed? Under the desk? Inside the wardrobe? No, not inside again. Under. Under the bed.

The boy went down the hall trying not to run and sneaked into the bedroom. Door open or closed? Just leave it. He crawled under his unmade bed, Spider-Man sheets spilling over the edge, and barricaded himself behind shoes, toys, and a hastily pulled duvet corner.

Then he listened.

Silence.

Was she done counting? How long did it take him to hide? It couldn't have been that long. Why wasn't she saying anything?

Was she even looking?

His breathing still loud from the hiding, he closed his eyes and tried to steady himself. He would hear something if he got quiet enough.

Nothing.

He should wait. That was the game.

But he wanted Mommy.

He made his way out from under the bed, swatting away a T-rex, an octopus, a firetruck with a missing wheel, and a white velcro shoe.

"Mommy! Mommy!"

He looked in the kitchen. In the bathroom. Mommy's room. Living room. Behind the fridge.

Where was she?

"Mommy?"
